{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~8–15 EUR), especially beyond ~day 5. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, larger for longer horizons.",
    "Trend/turning-point error: model extrapolates the prior uptrend while the market mean-reverts and sells off. When the last 30d show a strong run-up (new highs / steep slope), dampen the forecast drift toward flat-to-down rather than continued rise.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: immediately after local peaks (history ends near 82–84), the model predicts continued high/renewed strength; realized outcomes drop quickly. Add a ‘post-peak’ penalty: if last price is near the 30d high, force a decline over the next 1–2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-dependent correction: small adjustment for days 1–3, moderate for days 4–10, and largest for days 11–30 (where the overestimation compounds). Use an increasing downward correction with horizon (e.g., ramping).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: model keeps prices in a high band with mild oscillations; realized path shows deeper drawdowns. Increase downside responsiveness: when recent momentum turns negative (last 3–5d down), steepen near-term declines and lower the medium-term baseline.",
    "Cap unrealistic late-horizon rebounds: model often projects rebounds back to mid-80s late in the horizon; realized prices remain in low-70s/high-60s. Impose a ceiling tied to recent realized mean (e.g., do not exceed last-30d mean + small premium) unless there is fresh positive momentum."
  ]
}
